NEW: @FrancisSharoon and I wrote some research about active &amp; passive fund buying and their combined impact on prices. It what should be shocking to no-one. The stocks most bought by active &amp; passive funds over the last 3 years were FAR far more likely to outperform their peers. https://t.co/HZBSKgXYPv

This is coincidental return and buying(selling) by funds so the return and passive/active ownership changes are over the same 3 yr period. The question you're likely asking yourself after seeing this is: "what can i do to predict the stocks that will get bid up by both active and passive managers?" 

and to be honest that's like... half of investing anyway? Buying things before other people realize their "value" or simply before others come in an bid them up.

@FrancisSharoon we looked for a way to use this ownership data to predict returns &amp; it also gave us a somewhat predictable answer. For this we look at rolling 1 yr periods for pretty much all of the russell 3k. The more something was bought over a year, the worse its excess return next year https://t.co/3JVR5unVUZ

@FrancisSharoon Part of this is just reversion to the mean and overcrowding in action. For excess returns we compared stocks against the returns of its size peers. But this data was particularly compelling when you only look at small caps... https://t.co/XLLttIyl54

@FrancisSharoon We sliced and diced this a bunch of different ways -- all of which are in the research note linked below including everything here and a lot more. We're still doing a lot of work with this data set. https://t.co/TsFjBL5Kb6

